{%- comment -%}
  templates/index.liquid - Crema Italia storefront (POC3)

  A single-document SPA: every "page" below is a .page block, toggled client-side
  by showPage() in ci-storefront.js. Editorial copy is authored here in Liquid; the
  catalog grids, roaster profiles, product detail, cart and account views are filled
  at runtime from assets/ci-catalog.json (the baked-in test dataset).

  This renders only once Shopify password protection is OFF, or to a password-holder
  previewing the theme. While pre-launch, visitors still see templates/password.liquid.

  PROD: when the live store is built, the JS-rendered grids are replaced by
  real Liquid loops over {{ collection.products }} + crema_italia.* metafields, and the
  Roccia subscription UI binds to native selling_plan_groups (Loop). Integration points
  are flagged with <!-- LOOP --> / <!-- PROD --> comments.
{%- endcomment -%}

<!-- ============================== HOME ============================== -->
<!-- POC5 MODEL (2026-07-10, pending Steve's sign-off): story-first landing.
     Hero = questions -> payoff + single quiz CTA; sticky jump-chips; founder
     confession; model; shelves + contextual Sorpresa CTA; roasters; promise.
     Featured Tour removed from home; quiz is invitation-only (no auto-launch). -->
<div class="page active" id="page-home">
  <div class="hero"><div class="hero-inner">
    <img class="hero-logo" src="{{ 'ci-logo-knockout.png' | asset_url }}" alt="Crema Italia" width="700" height="490">
    {%- comment -%}
      Hero rewritten 2026-08-18. The old hero ASKED a question ("Do you love Espresso, Italy...?
      Or are you simply looking for delicious, fresh coffee beans?") and then answered it with a
      greeting. Three problems, all measured:
        1. It named TWO audiences, the second being the entire coffee-drinking population, which
           dilutes the first and makes a premium price read as expensive rather than as worth it.
        2. The actual answer - "we change nothing" - was the 214th word on the page, buried in
           paragraph 2 of section 3, even though it is the whole business and no competitor can
           say it.
        3. On a phone the question wrapped to FOUR lines against a one-line payoff, so the
           question carried ~4x the visual mass of the answer.
      Now: the claim leads, one audience is named, and Benvenuto stays as the warm second beat
      where a greeting belongs. Subhead is no longer italic - Marcellus has no italic face and
      the old one was a synthesised oblique (see the type block in ci-storefront.css).
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Revised again 2026-08-18 (Steve). Two changes and a consequence:
      (a) "Italian coffee" is a ROASTING STYLE, not a provenance or a date - Illy and Lavazza are
          "Italian coffee" and sit on a shelf for a year. Freshness is the strongest persuasive fact
          this brand has and it appeared NOWHERE above the fold; it was two screens down in "Our
          model" and on the Promise page, which already says "airfreight coffee freshly roasted in
          Italy" verbatim. So the headline now leads with it. The category word is not lost - the
          logo lockup directly above reads ITALIAN ARTISAN COFFEE, and the subhead says "Coffee
          from...". Measured: identical line count to the old headline (2 desktop / 3 mobile), so
          the freshness gain is free. Keep the H1 under ~59 characters or it tips to 4 lines at 375.
      (b) Two sentences, not a comma splice. "just as the roaster sealed it" can read temporally
          ("at the moment of sealing"); "Exactly as" cannot. Two declaratives also give each beat
          full weight, which suits the register better than a dependent clause.
      (c) CONSEQUENCE: the subhead used to say "change nothing... no re-roasting, no re-grinding,
          no re-bagging", which now merely repeats the H1. It was replaced with the MECHANISM that
          substantiates the claim (air-freight, weeks not months) rather than restating it.
      "grind their own beans", not "grind their own" - and note this is the only whole-bean signal
      above the fold, which is a partial answer to the open finding that nothing warns a buyer they
      need a grinder before this coffee is drinkable.
      "For PEOPLE who", not "for those who" (Steve asked, 2026-08-18): the Standard's voice is
      "plain, direct, deferential", and "for those who..." is the house construction of aspirational
      luxury copy - it flatters the reader into a discerning elite, which is adjacent to the
      "exclusive / world-class" register §6 bans.
      "who LOVE TO grind their own beans" (Steve, same day) - we are filtering for PASSION, not for
      capability. This replaced two weaker drafts. "...who grind their own beans and can taste the
      difference" was cut because discernment is an EXCLUSIVE claim (it implies others cannot taste
      it); "...who grind their own beans" was merely a hardware test. Affection is inclusive and
      still names the audience, and it echoes the About page's own Three P's: "we are a group
      passionate about coffee". Passion without elitism is the line to hold here.
      Watch the subhead's rendered line count - the ORIGINAL hero's defect was a subhead with ~4x
      the visual mass of the headline, and a long subhead recreates it. 5 lines at 375 is the cap.
      NOTE: "Freshly roasted" is now the most prominent claim on the site. If transit or the
      six-week arrival cadence slips, this headline is the first thing that becomes untrue.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <h1>A small, named group of roasters.<br class="h1-break">Whole bean, roasted in Italy.<br class="h1-break">Exactly as the roaster sealed it.</h1>
    <p class="subhead" style="max-width:58ch;margin:.85rem auto .35rem">We fly it in. Most bags sell within 14 to {{ settings.freshness_window_days }} days of roasting, and none after {{ settings.offerta_fresh_days }}. What doesn't sell goes to Feeding Tampa Bay. For people who love to grind their own beans.</p>
    <p class="hero-benvenuto">Benvenuto - welcome&nbsp;in.</p>
    <!-- The quiz is the hero CTA (Steve, 2026-08-06) - it is free, it is the lowest-commitment
         way in, and it is the only onboarding asset that works before real photography exists.
         This AMENDS the 2026-07-10 lock ("no hero button"; Sorpresa primary, quiz a quiet inline
         link). It is invitation-only - the first-visit auto-launch stays removed. -->
    <div class="hero-btns" style="margin-top:1.5rem"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="openQuiz()">Take our tasting quiz</button></div>
  </div></div>

  <!-- PROD: photography slot 1 of 3. TEMPORARY IMAGE - ci-temp-lp1.jpg is a visualization
       stand-in, NOT final art, and every ci-temp-* asset must be replaced before launch.
       BRIEF for the real shot: an Italian bar counter mid-morning service. Cups on saucers,
       steam, hands at work. Nobody posing. 21:9, ~2200x950, natural light, low saturation,
       narrow depth of field (Brand Standards 3.5).
       NOTE the stand-in is a US specialty cafe with an English chalkboard menu and dollar
       prices, so it reads American rather than Italian - fine for judging layout, wrong for
       the story this section tells. -->
  <div class="photo-slot photo-band is-filled" style="margin-top:1.75rem">
    <img src="{{ 'ci-temp-lp1.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="A cafe counter during morning service" width="1543" height="778">
  </div>

  <!-- Jump chips are now purely navigational: every one scrolls to a section. The quiz chip used
       to sit here, which made it the odd one out twice over - it opened a modal rather than
       scrolling, and its gold fill was byte-identical to .pill.active, so it read as "selected"
       rather than "selectable". It now lives in the hero above. -->
  <nav class="home-jump" aria-label="On this page">
    <button onclick="jumpHome('sec-story')">Our story</button>
    <button onclick="jumpHome('sec-shelves')">Shelves</button>
    <button onclick="jumpHome('sec-promise')">Promise</button>
  </nav>

  <main class="ci-main">
    <!-- Our story / founder confession -->
    <div class="section-head" id="sec-story" style="margin-top:0"><p class="eyebrow">Our story</p><h2>A full confession.</h2></div>
    <div class="story-grid">
      <div class="prose" style="max-width:72ch">
        {%- comment -%}
        UNITS: "17g" below is a DELIBERATE exception to Brand Standards §9 (both metric and
        U.S. customary on every weight reference). Everywhere else on the site now carries
        both. A brew dose is the one weight that is expressed in grams even in America -
        every US espresso recipe reads "18 g in, 36 g out" - so "17 g (0.60 oz)" would read
        as LESS fluent to the exact reader the hero names, "people who love to grind their
        own beans". It is also a sentence in a personal story, not a product spec.
        Same shape as the documented em-dash exception on the regions map. Do not "fix" it
        in a units sweep; if Steve wants it converted, convert it here only.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p>I&rsquo;ve fallen in love with Italy, and all things Italian - especially the caff&egrave; culture. On a recent trip to Tuscany a friend gave me a parting gift: a bag of decaf beans. I ground exactly 17g, pulled a shot (<span class="ita">un caff&egrave;</span>), and was blown away. So I looked for the roaster online to order more, and found they didn&rsquo;t ship.</p>
        <p>That shot was the inspiration, and it is now our mission: to find Italian roasters like that one and bring their coffee here, unchanged. Perhaps you have already discovered Italy and want to rekindle a memory. Maybe you simply want high-quality beans to grind and savor. Either way, I hope you find it here.</p>
        <img class="founder-signature" src="{{ 'ci-signature.png' | asset_url }}" alt="Steve Roberts" width="184" height="78" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        <p class="founder-sign">Steve Roberts, Founder</p>
      </div>
      <!-- PROD: photography slot 2 of 3. TEMPORARY IMAGE - ci-temp-lp2.jpg is a stand-in.
           BRIEF for the real shot: Steve at a caffe table in Campiglia or Sarteano. Candid,
           mid-conversation, not looking at the lens. 4:5, ~1000x1250.
           The story is written in the first person; a face is what earns that register. -->
      <div class="photo-slot photo-portrait is-filled">
        <img src="{{ 'ci-temp-lp2.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="Steve Roberts at a cafe table" width="1200" height="1600" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Our model -->
    <div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Our model</p><h2>Curated, never aggregated.</h2></div>
    <div class="prose" style="max-width:72ch">
      {%- comment -%}
        VOICE: this sentence used to read "We have carefully hand-selected a small number of
        roasters whose work represents Italian roasting at its finest." That is the Brand
        Standards' own named anti-pattern, inverted - §9 prints "We hand-pick the world's most
        exclusive coffee artisans" as the example NOT to write, and §6 of CLAUDE.md bans
        "hand-picked" and "world-class" outright. The replacement below is the Standard's
        approved construction, near-verbatim. Do not reintroduce a superlative here: the
        restraint IS the positioning.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p>We choose a small number of roasters whose work represents the craft of Italian roasting at its best. We do not roast, grind, blend, or alter the roasters&rsquo; packaging - instead, shortly after the roaster seals the whole-bean coffee into valve bags, we air-freight it directly into our US fulfillment facility, exactly as they produced and sealed it - fresh and ready to grind. We sell whole bean only, never pre-ground.</p>
      <p>Meet the full group on our <button class="inline-link" onclick="showPage('roasters')">Roasters page</button>. Do you have a roaster you&rsquo;d like us to consider? <a class="inline-link" href="mailto:roasters@cremaitalia.com">Email me here.</a></p>
    </div>
    <!-- PROD: photography slot 3 of 3. TEMPORARY IMAGE - ci-temp-lp3.jpg is a stand-in.
         BRIEF for the real shot: one of OUR roasters' own valve bags, sealed, label facing,
         close enough to read the roaster's name. Loose beans alongside. 3:2, ~1500x1000.
         This is the PRODUCT shot - the review's "no product visible anywhere on the landing
         page" - and it illustrates the claim it sits under: the roaster's own sealed bag,
         untouched by us.
         NOTE the stand-in shows third-party brands (Lavazza, a US roaster) - fine for layout,
         but it must not ship: it puts other companies' trademarks on our landing page, and
         Lavazza is mass-market, the opposite of the artisan sourcing this section claims. -->
    <div class="photo-slot photo-wide is-filled">
      <img src="{{ 'ci-temp-lp3.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="Sealed whole-bean coffee bags" width="1200" height="759" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    </div>

    <!-- Quiz offered BEFORE the catalog, not after the Sorpresa CTA (Steve, 2026-08-06). Placed
         here it reads as help arriving just as four shelves appear; after the CTA it read as a
         fallback for people who had already declined to buy. -->
    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:2rem"><strong>Find a roast to love.</strong> Take our <button class="inline-link" onclick="openQuiz()">three-question quiz</button> and we will show you the coffees that match your taste.</div>

    <!-- Shelves -->
    <div class="section-head" id="sec-shelves"><p class="eyebrow">How the catalog is organized</p><h2>The four coffee shelves.</h2></div>
    <p class="prose" style="max-width:72ch">One shelf for subscriptions and larger formats (<span class="ita">Roccia</span>), one for discovery and gifting (<span class="ita">Sorpresa</span>), one for rare or seasonal roasts (<span class="ita">Selezione</span>), and one for last-chance lots at an honest markdown (<span class="ita">Offerta</span>).</p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="shelf-card" onclick="showPage('roccia')"><h3>Roccia</h3><div class="ss2">The Rock · Subscription (and one-time ordering)</div><p>Your preferred coffee, size, and cadence. Subscribe for free shipping and a 10% discount.</p></div>
      <div class="shelf-card" onclick="showPage('sorpresa')"><h3>Sorpresa</h3><div class="ss2">The Surprise · Curated Discovery</div><p>Our curated collections of roasted delights. On-demand 100 g (3.53 oz) collections - the best way to find your favorite roast subscription.</p></div>
      <div class="shelf-card" onclick="showPage('selezione')"><h3>Selezione</h3><div class="ss2">The Selection · Premium &amp; Seasonal</div><p>Roasters' premium and seasonal offerings. Micro-lots and limited imports. When a product sells out, it may not return - once it's gone, it's gone.</p></div>
      <div class="shelf-card" onclick="showPage('offerta')"><h3>Offerta</h3><div class="ss2">The Offer · Limited Time and Inventory</div><p>Limited-time offers, on sale while inventory lasts. Good coffee approaching its freshness window, priced honestly. Sold as-is.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1.75rem"><strong>Your first purchase is our introduction.</strong> You may already know the roaster by reputation and are ready to subscribe. If not, try Sorpresa - we&rsquo;ll introduce you to a roaster you&rsquo;ll fall in love with, and hope that a Roccia subscription will soon follow.</div>
    <div class="hero-btns" style="margin-top:1.25rem"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="showPage('sorpresa')">Begin with a Sorpresa collection</button></div>

    <!-- Promise -->
    <div class="section-head" id="sec-promise"><p class="eyebrow">The Crema Italia Promise</p><h2>We deliver on these promises.</h2></div>
    <ul class="promise-list">
      <li><div class="pn">1</div><div class="pt"><h3>Curated with love, never aggregated.</h3><p>A small group of Italian roasters - carefully chosen, not collected.</p></div></li>
      <li><div class="pn">2</div><div class="pt"><h3>Love your first bag, or we send a different one - free.</h3><p>On Roccia, Sorpresa, and Selezione. Offerta is sold as-is (see <button class="inline-link" onclick="showPage('promise')">the Promise page</button>).</p></div></li>
      <li><div class="pn">3</div><div class="pt"><h3>Cancel your subscription anytime in a couple of clicks.</h3><p>Pause, skip, swap, or cancel - all self-service, no minimum, no cancel fee.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </main>
</div>

<!-- ============================== SHOP ============================== -->
<div class="page" id="page-shop">
  <!-- Brown hero: catalog framing + EPHEMERAL Shelf/Region navigation. The taste axis
       (the saved profile) lives in the global ribbon above the hero + the taste console
       modal it opens ("Edit profile") - not shown inline here anymore. -->
  <div class="dark-hero"><div class="dark-hero-inner">
    <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--ci-crema-light)">All shelves</p>
    <h1>Shop all our Coffee</h1>
    <div class="hero-filters">
      <!-- The shelf note to the right of the pills names what the current selection means, so
           the four Italian shelf names teach themselves as you browse. Text is READ FROM the
           matching shelf page's own .sub at selection time (shelfNote() in ci-storefront.js),
           so there is exactly one description per shelf and nothing to keep in sync. It used
           to be a parallel SHELF_NOTE table and it had already drifted. Edit the shelf page. -->
      <div class="filter-group"><div class="filter-label">Shelf</div><div class="filter-pills">
        <span class="pill active" onclick="filterShelf(this,'all')">All</span>
        <span class="pill" onclick="filterShelf(this,'roccia')">Roccia</span>
        <span class="pill" onclick="filterShelf(this,'sorpresa')">Sorpresa</span>
        <span class="pill" onclick="filterShelf(this,'selezione')">Selezione</span>
        <span class="pill" onclick="filterShelf(this,'offerta')">Offerta</span>
        <span class="shelf-note" id="shelf-note">Every coffee we carry.</span>
      </div></div>
      <div class="filter-group filter-region">
        <div class="filter-label">Region</div>
        {% render 'ci-region-filter' %}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div></div>
  <!-- Cream body: the grid. -->
  <main class="ci-main">
    <div class="card-grid" id="shop-grid"></div>
    <div id="no-results" style="display:none;text-align:center;padding:3rem 1rem;color:var(--ci-mute)">
      <p style="font-weight:600;color:var(--ci-espresso)">No coffees match these filters right now.</p>
      <p style="font-size:.9rem;margin-top:.25rem">Try widening your taste profile, or <button onclick="clearProfile()" style="background:none;border:none;color:var(--ci-crema);font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;text-decoration:underline">clear filters</button>.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</div>

<!-- ============================== ROCCIA ============================== -->
<div class="page" id="page-roccia">
  <div class="dark-hero"><div class="dark-hero-inner">
    <button class="back-btn" style="color:var(--ci-crema-light)" onclick="goBack()">&larr; Back</button>
    <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--ci-crema-light)">The Rock · Subscription (and one-time ordering)</p>
    <h1>Roccia</h1>
    <p class="sub">Our rock-solid subscription plan.</p>
    <p>Choose any roast from any roaster and receive it on your cadence - every 4, 6, or 8 weeks. Bag sizes 250 g (8.82 oz), 500 g (1.10 lb), and 1 kg (2.20 lb). All subscriptions ship free within the U.S.</p>
    <div class="cn"><strong>Self-service, always.</strong> Pause anytime, skip anytime, swap roaster or bag up to 48 hours before the order locks, cancel anytime. No minimum commitment. No cancel fee.</div>
  </div></div>
  <main class="ci-main">
    <div class="callout">Your subscriber benefit is <strong>10% off Roccia, Sorpresa, and Selezione</strong> - on your subscription shipments, and on anything else you order from those three shelves. <strong>Offerta and Bottega are priced as-is and are never discounted.</strong></div>
    <div class="founding-banner"><strong>Founding Members</strong> - the first {{ settings.founding_member_cap }} subscribers receive 12% instead of 10%. Founding Membership is limited to {{ settings.founding_member_cap }} and does not reopen.</div>
    <div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Available now · Toscana</p><h2>Roccia coffees</h2></div>
    <div class="card-grid" id="grid-roccia"></div>
    <div class="afd">Fresh coffee arrives from Italy about every six weeks. If we are short on your coffee when your order comes due, we will tell you before it ships and let you choose: wait for the new arrival, or let us substitute something close from the same shelf. Either way, never at extra cost.</div>
  </main>
</div>

<!-- ============================== SORPRESA ============================== -->
<div class="page" id="page-sorpresa">
  <div class="dark-hero"><div class="dark-hero-inner">
    <button class="back-btn" style="color:var(--ci-crema-light)" onclick="goBack()">&larr; Back</button>
    <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--ci-crema-light)">The Surprise · Curated Discovery</p>
    <h1>Sorpresa</h1>
    <p class="sub">Our curated collections of roasted delights.</p>
    <p>Named collections, our choice of three different 100 g (3.53 oz) bags assembled on-demand so every bag is fresh when it reaches you. Each collection includes a printed tasting card naming each roaster in the collection and their tasting notes. The aim of every collection is simple: to help you discover your favorite roasters and roasts so that you can add them to your Roccia subscription plan, or gift to your coffee-loving friends.</p>
  </div></div>
  <main class="ci-main">
    <!-- Catalog-driven, like every other shelf (Steve, 2026-08-19). This was a hard-coded
         .tour-hero spotlighting one fixture Tour - its handle, title, price ($77.70) and
         component list all written into the template, with a View Tour button opening that
         one SKU. Sorpresa was the only shelf without a grid. Steve: "let the user read about
         whatever tours are currently offered and choose one of their liking" - so however
         many collections exist, they all show, and none of them is named here. -->
    <div class="section-head" style="margin-top:0"><p class="eyebrow">Assembled fresh</p><h2>Sorpresa Collections</h2></div>
    <div class="card-grid" id="grid-sorpresa"></div>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Why a collection sometimes disappears.</strong> A collection is only available while all three of its coffees are in stock and within our freshly roasted promise window. If we do have to swap a coffee after you order, we pair you with something close from the same roaster and say so in your confirmation.</div>
    <div class="afd">The selection is ours, not the roaster's, because curating is our job. Every collection is three 100 g bags, never mixed sizes.</div>
  </main>
</div>

<!-- ============================== SELEZIONE ============================== -->
